  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{{tan}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1}-1.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1}-1=\\frac{{sec}^{2}\u2061x-1}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}\u2061x-1}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1}-1 & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1)(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x-1)}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1)}(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x-1)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1}}-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x-1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1}-1=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x-2.\n$$",
